From 01a8fe0b30533653d59c46d62dcd3c53194053fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:47:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(worker): derive the worker receipt bound from the owner's
 write patience; log slow worker.persist [daveey]

Problem: David-MBP lost 3 Kanban worker runs on 10-30 (11:56, 12:44 PDT) to
`WorkerPersistenceError: worker_receipt_timeout`. The worker waited a literal
20 s (`link.receive(20)`) for a `worker.persist` reply while the owner may wait
up to `_TRANSCRIPT_WRITE_PATIENCE_S` (60 s) for the state.db write lock, and
then commit the write anyway. The worker never resends (outcome unknown), so a
slow but successful owner write killed the run.

Why 1: finalize_turn's persist got no reply inside 20 s.
Why 2: the owner was still inside its 20/60 s write patience (load 150, 26
       Hermes processes on one state.db; owner loop stalled >10 s at 11:56).
Root (happened): client and server bounds of one contract were set
       independently; the client's was not longer than the server's.
Root (not caught): no test paired the two bounds, and the owner logged nothing
       for a slow worker.persist, so stalls were invisible until a worker died.

Fix:
- hermes_state_runtime.worker_receipt_timeout_s(): the longest owner write
  patience of any worker.register/adopt/persist handler + 30 s margin
  (WORKER_RECEIPT_MARGIN_S; owner-loop stalls) = 90 s today. WorkerRPC uses it.
- gateway.session_worker.timed_persist: one WARNING per worker.persist slower
  than 5 s with operation, session, state.db lock wait (recorded by
  apply_worker_receipt through WORKER_RECEIPT_TIMING), total and outcome.

Tests (RED on main, and each fails under its mutation: `receive(20)` back, or
the log line dropped):
- tests/agent/test_worker_receipt_bound_contract.py: the bound outlasts every
  patience the real register/adopt/persist handlers pass to _execute_write;
  the derivation; the slow-persist WARNING fields; no WARNING when fast.
- tests/gateway/test_worker_slow_owner_persist.py: a real owner daemon + real
  worker AIAgent; a foreign writer holds the state.db write lock for 25 s
  mid-turn. The worker exits 0, its assistant row is stored once, and the
  owner logs exactly one slow-persist WARNING (lock_wait/total > 20 s).
